WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.870 align:start position:0%
 
com  c-span  join american history tv this weekend as  we visit charleston south carolina  we're visiting one southeastern city  each month to learn more about its rich  history  our next charleston stop is the old  slave market to hear about one of the  largest slave trading and selling hubs  in the confederacy  we are sitting in the former sales room  for the ryan smart auction complex  we're up on the second floor but  originally this building didn't have a  second floor there was just a long  narrow  building that was part of a complex of  four buildings that made up ryan's mart  we know that charleston played a very  significant role during the  transatlantic slave trade  and it was a major port of entry for  enslaved africans coming into what is  now the united states  about 40 percent of all enslaved  africans coming into this country  in 18th century entered through the port  of charleston  but the transatlantic slave trade was  abolished in 1808 although there was  much smuggling  but what we focus on here is the  domestic slave trade since the majority  of enslaved people who  were sold here at ryan's more were  american born they were the children  grandchildren and great-grandchildren of  those enslaved africans that had been  brought here against their world  generations before in 1856 when we see  the first advertised slave sale here  the 20 year old woman named lucinda this  building wasn't even here and if you  look at the walls around us you'll  notice that  this was actually an alley it was an  alley that led into the larger auction  complex and there were three buildings  there the main building  was a four-story jail or barracoon and  that's where enslaved people were held  before sale  there was also a two-story structure  that was used as a kitchen there was  also a smaller structure in the  southwest corner of the lot  that was described as a dead house or a  morgue and if the slave trader knew that  there was an enslaved person in that  jail that was sick they would remove  that person to this other structure so  as not to  infect the  larger population thousands of enslaved  people were sold here between 1856 and  1863.  the largest  slave sale that i know about that was  advertised was 256 enslaved people  in  today's dollars  a very  enslaved man who is in the prime of his  life very strong and able-bodied could  be sold for  in today's about 35 000  and of course if you had a special trade  or skill you would be worth more  enslaved people had to be  prepared for sale  especially if they  had traveled from a great distance if  an enslaved person let's say they were  coming from  richmond virginia  and coming here to ryan smart to be sold  that could take  weeks or months  traveling on a good day about 20 miles  20 to 25 miles a day so it could take a  long time and by the time they got here  to ryan smart they definitely wouldn't  be in any condition to be sold they  would be bruised probably with  with the shackles and chains they'd been  wearing especially the men who were  shackled the entire time  uh very thin  uh dirty  they were in no position to be sold on  the auction block a potential buyer  um  wouldn't  wouldn't want to purchase them so  arriving in a place like this this  auction complex  thomas ryan and then later zb oaks would  have a staff  of uh black and white enslaved and free  people here working to get  those people who were coming to be sold  ready for the auction block enslaved  people were fed very well before auction  trying to fatten them up  they would also be provided soap and  water here at ryan's mart to  wash themselves and sometimes  they would also be given palm oil or  some other kind of oil and encouraged to  rub that into their skin  so  that  they could appear healthier and their  muscles would look more defined and they  would look bulked up so all that would  happen and of course the last thing that  had to happen  was that people had to  find out when that auction was was going  to happen  and  so ads would have been placed in the  local papers  and some of those larger slave auctions  would be advertised throughout the  southeast  so if you were a slave trader in the  south and someone said ryan smart you  would know they would know  where you were talking about  enslaved people you would think that  they wouldn't have any agency they  wouldn't have any power in a situation  like this or maybe as they stood on an  auction block no power  um  they knew they were going to be sold but  there were things that they did for  instance if there was a  enslaved man who was  on the auction block and  a  potential buyer started bidding on him  a potential buyer who he knew of  maybe he would start speaking up and  saying you know if you buy me  um i will work for you i work very hard  for you sir and and maybe start bragging  about how how strong he was or or  um  how skilled he was and maybe even try to  encourage that  buyer  to purchase his wife and child also and  and so enslaved people did that and it  wasn't because they were happy with  their  their  um  lot in life or happy about being  enslaved or content  this man was trying to keep his family  together and he was going to do anything  and say anything to  to achieve that  the last advertised slave sale  here at ryan's mart occurred in november  1863 and it wasn't until about a  1936 that miriam wilson  purchased the building  and she  knew and many people knew that this was  a place where enslaved people were sold  and she wanted to  open this building as a museum  so she established the old slave mart  museum  in 1937 and um opened i believe in  march of that year so opened this  building as the first african-american  museum in the country  all weekend american history tv brings  you the history of charleston south  carolina  to learn more about our tour of eight  southeastern cities  visit cspan.org  local content  we're standing in a place that we call  chin ridge it's the farm of benjamin  chin

